# Known Limitations

## Experimental status

The current methodology remains experimental.

NeoMundi does not claim:
- universal runtime interpretability,
- universal hallucination detection,
- or complete semantic verification capabilities.

The objective is to explore whether constrained runtime evidence structures can support meaningful governance instrumentation.

---

# Fluent hallucinations

Some outputs may remain thermodynamically stable while still containing factually incorrect information.

This class of failure is currently referred to as:
- fluent hallucinations.

Such outputs may present:
- low runtime variation,
- stable continuity patterns,
- and no observable instability signals.

This limitation is explicitly recognized.

---

# Semantic-blind constraints

The OBS methodology intentionally operates under semantic-blind constraints.

As a consequence:
- certain semantic inconsistencies,
- contextual contradictions,
- or factual errors

may remain outside direct runtime observability.

This tradeoff is intentional and linked to:
- privacy,
- non-retention,
- and independent runtime instrumentation goals.

---

# Model dependency

Runtime observations remain partially dependent on:
- provider architectures,
- inference stacks,
- model versions,
- and execution conditions.

Observed patterns may vary between providers and runtime environments.

---

# Protocol dependency

Results remain dependent on:
- protocol design,
- prompt construction,
- execution constraints,
- and observer separation quality.

Poorly constrained protocols may introduce circular validation patterns.

---

# No universal truth verification

NeoMundi does not currently claim to verify factual truth.

The methodology focuses on:
- runtime continuity,
- behavioral regime transitions,
- and observable execution patterns.

Truth verification remains a separate problem space.

---

# No claim of physical law validation

NeoMundi does not claim to demonstrate or validate a universal physical law governing AI systems.

The methodology explores whether:
- variation-based runtime measurements,
- continuity observations,
- and constrained evidence structures

can support operational governance instrumentation.

---

# Ongoing methodological evolution

The methodology continues to evolve through:
- runtime experiments,
- benchmark comparisons,
- observer-separation protocols,
- and independently reviewable evaluations.

Some components remain exploratory and subject to revision.
